  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows scattered showers\nand storms moving east at around 15mph mainly offshore the \nVolusia to Brevard county coasts, in addition to, over the \nTreasure Coast. A few strong storms have developed which have \nproduced wind gusts up to 43mph (radar indicated up to 60mph at \nCape Canaveral earlier). GOES-16 satellite imagery and local \nobservations indicate partly to mostly cloudy skies with \nstratocumulus and cumulonimbus clouds moving east, as well as \ncirrus clouds moving south. Analysis charts show a stationary \nboundary over the deep south with a mid/upper level trough over \nthe western Atlantic and northeastern Florida. The 18:30Z MCO \nACARS sounding indicates MLCAPE at 1,446 J/kg, DCAPE at 961 J/kg, \nPWAT value of 2.002\", and weak shear. Additionally, the MDPI value\nis 1.0 today. Temperatures are currently in the mid 80s to low \n90s with heat index values up to 100F-108F degrees (hottest over \nMartin and Okeechobee counties). Muggy conditions are in place \nacross east central Florida with dew points in the mid 70s to \naround 80F degrees. \n\nScattered to numerous showers (60-80% chance) are forecast across\neast central Florida this afternoon and early evening before rain\nchances diminish around 7PM from northwest to southeast. \nScattered storms will be capable of wind gusts up to 40-50mph \n(less than 5% chance of a storm or two capable of wind gusts up to\n60mph), frequent lightning, and heavy rainfall (isolated amounts \nof 1-3\" with a 5-14% chance of isolated amounts up to 3-5\"). For \nthis reason a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall exists for east\ncentral Florida today with the potential for flooding of roadways\nand low-lying areas. Mostly dry weather is forecast overnight \nwith lows in the low to mid 70s.\n\nThe Weekend... A shortwave trough is expected to develop over the\nFlorida panhandle and eastern Gulf on Saturday and Sunday. Rain \nchances (60-80% chance) remain high through the weekend with PWATs\nin excess of 2\" coupled with increased lift for shower and \nlightning storm development. Scattered to numerous showers and \nlightning storms are forecast to develop each afternoon and \nevening as a weak east coast sea breeze backs onshore mainly along\nthe coastal counties and converges with southwest surface winds \non Saturday with the sea breeze, as well as outflow from previous \nstorms. Gradient winds are expected to increase on Sunday with \n850mb winds up to 25-35kts. Surface winds at 10-15mph from the \nsouthwest with gusts to 20-25mph are forecast which will likely \nprevent the east coast sea breeze from developing north of Cape \nCanaveral and will keep the greatest rain chances from Cape \nCanaveral southwards on Sunday. \n\nThe Atlantic ridge is expected to build over Florida on Sunday. \nModel guidance shows MLCAPE up to 1,500-3,200 J/kg, 0-6km shear up\nto 20-45kts on Saturday, as well a 15-35kts on Sunday. The main \nhazards with isolated to scattered strong to marginally severe \nstorms will be wind gusts up to 40-50mph (less than 5% chance of a\nstorm or two capable of wind gusts up to 60mph on Sunday). A \nMarginal Risk for excessive rainfall exists on Saturday (isolated \namounts up to 1-3\" with a 5% chance of isolated rainfall amounts \nup to 3-4\" with the potential for flooding of roadways and low-\nlying areas). Near normal afternoon highs in the upper 80s to low \n90s and lows in the low to mid 70s are forecast.\n\nMonday-Thursday... Higher than normal rain chances continue into\nMonday with PWATs in excess of 2\". The Atlantic ridge is expected\nto continue to build over Florida into next week which will \nresult in a return to closer to normal rain chances on Tuesday and\nWednesday. Winds are forecast to weaken from the southwest at \n5-10mph on Monday, back south on Tuesday, and back east on \nWednesday with onshore flow in place through Thursday. The east \ncoast sea breeze is forecast to develop in the afternoon along the\ncoast on Monday and across most of east central Florida Tuesday \nafternoon and evening. Isolated to scattered lightning storms will\nbe capable of frequent lightning, gusty winds, and heavy \nrainfall. Near normal afternoon highs in the upper 80s to low 90s \nwith lows generally in the low to mid 70s are forecast.",
